WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.120
Good morning, Hank, it's Tuesday!

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:07.100
NerdCon in Boston was so amazing that I barely took any videos,

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:12.500
so I'm just gonna talk over this footage of Hank Green and the Perfect Strangers rocking out to songs about science.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.160
I want to try to say now what I struggled to say during the NerdCon closing ceremonies:

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.560
So, I was thinking about Esther a lot this weekend.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:22.900
Esther Earl was a Nerdfighter, who, with her friends, helped run the behind-the-scenes of the project for awesome,

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:25.780
and she died of Cancer in 2010 when she was sixteen.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:30.260
Esther and I met in Boston, and her Make A Wish brought some of her Nerdfighter friends and I to Boston,

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:34.960
and now, here we were again, dancing and singing along to songs Esther loved.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:38.280
On Saturday, I visited her grave with her parents and one of her sisters-

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:40.800
A lot of Nerdfighters have visited over the years, actually,

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.200
and left artwork, and keychains, and This Star Won't Go Out bracelets

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:49.080
and I was thinking about how Esther met her Nerdfighter friends in the comments of our old BlogTV live shows,

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:52.880
like, after the shows ended they would stay in the chat room and talk to each other.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:56.500
Their friendship basically happened in a space that we created,

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:57.520
and then vacated.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:01.800
Hank, when I look back at the last ten years of Vlogbrothers, I don't really think about the videos.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.900
Malcom Gladwell famously said that it takes 10,000 hours of practice to become expert at something,

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:11.800
but even after far more than 10,000 hours, I'm still not really good at making YouTube videos.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.100
I mean, I can manage a jump cut, but that's about it.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:15.940
See I don't really think about the videos -

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.000
I think about people making stuff together and using that stuff to connect with each other.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.360
I think about an Amsterdam Nerdfighter gathering

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:25.360
in 2008 where two people met, who would later get married.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:27.360
I think about the Project for Awesome, and scavenger hunts...

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:33.560
and about how Vlogbrothers is one of the very few channels on YouTube where the comments are often better than the videos.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:36.120
Ultimately, fandoms are made interesting not by the content,

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:39.880
but by the people who choose to build communities around the content.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:41.640
Like Harry Potter is an excellent book series,

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:44.480
but what makes it special is finally not J.K. Rowling...

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.760
but readers choosing to respond deeply and enthusiastically...

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:52.400
and without embarrassment or apology by dressing up or writing and reading fanfiction

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:55.080
or singing that the weapon we have is love.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:02:00.860
Hank, we've been incredibly fortunate over the last decade that so many awesome people have chosen to invest themselves in Nerdfighteria.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:02.860
Sometimes I feel guilty about it...

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:07.300
because I've benefited so disproportionately from something I only  had a small part in creating.

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.860
Each Tuesday I try to repay a little bit of that debt,

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:11.300
but it only seems to grow.

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:12.860
Which brings me back to Esther.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:15.540
At her grave I kept wondering how you thank someone who is gone...

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:20.240
how you repay them for the deep lasting gifts that their love and friendship brought

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:22.560
you when you can no longer do anything for them.

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:23.400
She isn't here.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.400
When I've lost people I care about,

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:26.980
that part never stops feeling new.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.040
They aren't here.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:30.840
It cracks me open each time I think it.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.280
She died is a past tense problem.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:36.100
She isn't here is a present tense problem.

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:39.640
But you are here, whoever you are however you came upon this video...

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:42.120
you are here and we're lucky to have you here...

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:48.240
and grateful, and while we are here together let's try to be kind and to create spaces where cool stuff can happen.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:50.000
So, Nerdfighters, thank you for your comments,

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.000
and your art, and your writing,

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.700
and your collaborations, and all the awesome you have brought into the world in the last ten years.

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:58.020
Hank, I will see you on Friday.

